---
import "../styles/global.css";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Progwise is building the next-generation platform for developers and their customers to create, maintain, and evolve software with AI agents, engineering guardrails, and human expertise."
    />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#ffffff" />
    <meta
      property="og:title"
      content="Progwise — You shape. Agents build. Experts guide."
    />
    <meta
      property="og:description"
      content="A shared platform for developers and their customers to create, maintain, and evolve software. AI agents carry the work forward, with engineering standards, guided decisions, and human expertise built into the process."
    />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://www.progwise.net/" />
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.progwise.net/" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <title>Progwise — You shape. Agents build. Experts guide.</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <a class="skip" href="#main">
      Skip to content
    </a>
    <header class="wrap">
      <a class="brand" href="#" aria-label="Progwise home">
        progwise
      </a>
      <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
        <a href="#platform">The platform</a>
        <a href="#how">How it works</a>
        <a class="nav-contact" href="#contact">
          Let’s talk
        </a>
      </nav>
    </header>
    <main id="main">
      <section class="hero wrap">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">The next chapter of building software</p>
          <h1>You shape. Agents build. Experts guide.</h1>
          <p class="intro">
            A shared platform for developers and their customers to create,
            maintain, and evolve software. AI agents carry the work forward,
            with engineering standards, guided decisions, and human expertise
            built into the process.
          </p>
          <p class="status">
            A platform in development. An invitation to shape it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="statement">
        <div class="wrap statement-inner">
          <p>
            Our ambition is a first version in days, followed by years of
            continuous improvement.
            <span>
              Designed for faster delivery and lower costs throughout the
              software lifecycle.
            </span>
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="wrap section" id="platform">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <h2>Your delivery process. Your customer’s workspace.</h2>
          <p>
            Progwise is a SaaS platform in development for independent
            developers, software companies, and the businesses they serve. Set
            up a customer project once, then keep its conversations, decisions,
            code, tests, releases, and costs connected as it grows.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="audiences">
          <article>
            <h3>For developers and software companies</h3>
            <p>
              Create customer workspaces, connect or create Git repositories,
              and configure agents, permissions, reviews, tests, and deployment
              environments. Bring your engineering standards to every project.
            </p>
            <p>
              Invite customers and designers into the process. Oversee projects,
              handle technical decisions, and step in where specialist judgment
              is needed. Decisions and delivery history would stay available to
              everyone who joins.
            </p>
            <a href="mailto:info@progwise.net?subject=Progwise%20for%20my%20customer%20projects">
              Discuss your customer projects
            </a>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>For business people and designers</h3>
            <p>
              Define what you need, explore suggestions, try previews, and
              approve or decline changes. Your developer establishes the
              engineering process. Progwise would guide you through the business
              decisions.
            </p>
            <p>
              After launch, request improvements in ordinary language. Agents
              would work through the project’s established checks and release
              process, bringing in your development team when expertise is
              needed.
            </p>
            <a href="mailto:info@progwise.net?subject=Evolving%20my%20software%20with%20Progwise">
              Discuss your software needs
            </a>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="wrap section" id="how">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <h2>Your business evolves. Your software comes with you.</h2>
          <p>
            A project stays with you through discovery, implementation, launch,
            and maintenance. Business users decide what the software should do.
            Developers establish how it is engineered. Progwise would coordinate
            the agents, decisions, and delivery across that entire lifecycle.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="steps">
          <article>
            <h3>Define what you need.</h3>
            <p>
              Share an idea or describe a problem in your own words. Progwise
              would listen, ask follow-up questions, suggest possibilities, and
              prepare clear decisions. Approve, decline, or refine the proposal
              until it fits.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>The right expertise joins.</h3>
            <p>
              Progwise would recommend and engage AI and human specialists
              through the project’s configured process and approved budget. Your
              development team can bring in design, web, database, or scaling
              expertise, with the context and expected costs already attached.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Keep making it yours.</h3>
            <p>
              After launch, type what needs to change. Progwise would use the
              project’s history to clarify the request, prepare a preview, test,
              and release it. Everyday improvements should be manageable by your
              business team, without hiring a developer for each change.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="technical wrap" id="design">
        <div class="technical-copy">
          <h2>Type what you need. Shape what happens next.</h2>
          <p>
            When your business expands, your AI consultant would use the
            project’s history to ask who needs access, suggest an approach, and
            turn your answers into a change you can try.
          </p>
          <p>
            You review the business outcome and approve the change. Behind it,
            agents would prepare code changes for review, run checks, and follow
            the project’s deployment process. Your development team and its
            specialists handle technical decisions, with business tradeoffs
            brought back to you.
          </p>
          <details>
            <summary>
              How would existing software and data be protected?
            </summary>
            <p>
              The planned safeguards include limited agent permissions, isolated
              previews, code review, and automated tests. Changes to shared
              services, sign-in, databases, and infrastructure would receive
              specialist review.
            </p>
            <p>
              Protecting existing data is part of the delivery process. Backups,
              verified recovery procedures, and tested data migrations would
              support changes. Release plans would cover monitoring and recovery
              for both software and data, so reverting code is never assumed to
              undo a database change.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="wrap section split">
        <div>
          <h2>Fund a month. See what moves forward.</h2>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="lead">
            Customer project work is planned around monthly funding, paid
            upfront. The customer and development provider agree the budget and
            priorities. Funded work begins when payment arrives.
          </p>
          <p>
            Progwise would track spending and committed costs, suggest
            priorities and specialists, and warn when funds run low. Additional
            spending needs your agreement, and further chargeable work pauses
            when the funded budget is exhausted.
          </p>
          <p>
            The full project has an evolving forecast for investment and timing.
            See what changed and why, alongside the expected cost of running and
            maintaining your software.
          </p>
          <p>
            Project funding is separate from the platform subscription for
            developers and software companies. Platform pricing is still being
            developed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="wrap section split meetings" id="meetings">
        <div>
          <h2>Walk in. Work it through together.</h2>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="lead">
            Every meeting would take place in the project office—a shared space
            with rooms and people you can walk up to.
          </p>
          <p>
            Each customer project would have its own office. Meet your developer
            and AI consultant, invite a human expert, or review a working
            preview together. Talk through ideas, designs, and decisions in the
            same place where the work happens.
          </p>
          <p>
            Progwise would turn the conversation into a summary you can correct,
            decisions you can approve, and work the team can carry forward.
            Between meetings, type a request to continue the work.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="contact" id="contact">
        <div class="wrap contact-inner">
          <h2>What should your software do next?</h2>
          <p>
            Run a development company? Build software for customers? Shape the
            tools your business uses? We’d like to hear how you work. Help us
            build the next generation of software project management for people
            and AI.
          </p>
          <a
            class="button light"
            href="mailto:info@progwise.net?subject=The%20next%20chapter%20of%20building%20software"
          >
            info@progwise.net
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
    <footer class="wrap">
      <a class="brand" href="#">
        progwise
      </a>
      <span>© {new Date().getFullYear()} Progwise Ltd.</span>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
